Creating an After Effects UI panel and plugin that integrates with the ElevenLabs API involves several steps, including setting up the UI, handling API requests, and managing the audio files in After Effects. Below is a high-level overview of how to achieve this, along with some sample code snippets.

### Prerequisites

1. **Adobe After Effects**: Ensure you have After Effects installed.
2. **Adobe ExtendScript Toolkit**: This is used for scripting in After Effects.
3. **ElevenLabs API Key**: You need to sign up for ElevenLabs and obtain an API key.

### Step 1: Create the UI Panel

You can create a simple UI panel using HTML and JavaScript. The panel will include:

- A login section for API credentials.
- A dropdown to select voices.
- A text input field for the text to be converted to speech.
- A button to submit the request.
- A checkbox for the "3x" option.

Here’s a basic structure for the HTML:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>ElevenLabs Voice Generator</title>
    <style>
        /* Add your styles here */
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div>
        <h1>ElevenLabs Voice Generator</h1>
        <input type="text" id="apiKey" placeholder="API Key" />
        <select id="voiceSelect"></select>
        <textarea id="textInput" placeholder="Enter text here..."></textarea>
        <button id="generateButton">Generate Voice</button>
        <input type="checkbox" id="threeXOption" /> 3x
    </div>
    <script src="panel.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Step 2: Handle API Requests

In your `panel.js`, you will handle the API requests to ElevenLabs. You will need to fetch the available voices and send requests to generate audio.

Here’s a basic example of how to fetch voices and send a request:

```javascript
const apiKeyInput = document.getElementById('apiKey');
const voiceSelect = document.getElementById('voiceSelect');
const textInput = document.getElementById('textInput');
const generateButton = document.getElementById('generateButton');
const threeXOption = document.getElementById('threeXOption');

async function fetchVoices() {
    const response = await fetch('https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1/voices', {
        headers: {
            'Authorization': `Bearer ${apiKeyInput.value}`
        }
    });
    const data = await response.json();
    populateVoiceSelect(data.voices);
}

function populateVoiceSelect(voices) {
    voices.forEach(voice => {
        const option = document.createElement('option');
        option.value = voice.id;
        option.textContent = voice.name;
        voiceSelect.appendChild(option);
    });
}

async function generateVoice() {
    const text = textInput.value;
    const voiceId = voiceSelect.value;
    const requests = threeXOption.checked ? [1, 2, 3] : [1];

    for (const request of requests) {
        const response = await fetch('https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1/text-to-speech', {
            method: 'POST',
            headers: {
                'Authorization': `Bearer ${apiKeyInput.value}`,
                'Content-Type': 'application/json'
            },
            body: JSON.stringify({
                text: text,
                voice: voiceId
            })
        });
        const audioData = await response.json();
        downloadAudio(audioData.audio_url, `output_${request}.mp3`);
    }
}

function downloadAudio(url, filename) {
    const a = document.createElement('a');
    a.href = url;
    a.download = filename;
    document.body.appendChild(a);
    a.click();
    document.body.removeChild(a);
}

generateButton.addEventListener('click', generateVoice);
fetchVoices();
```

### Step 3: Insert Audio into After Effects Timeline

To insert the downloaded MP3 files into After Effects, you will need to use ExtendScript. You can create a script that runs after the audio files are downloaded.

Here’s a simple ExtendScript example to import audio files:

```javascript
function importAudio(filePath) {
    var importOptions = new ImportOptions(File(filePath));
    var audioFile = app.project.importFile(importOptions);
    return audioFile;
}

function addAudioToTimeline(audioFile) {
    var comp = app.project.items.addComp("Generated Audio", 1920, 1080, 1, 10, 30);
    var layer = comp.layers.add(audioFile);
    layer.startTime = 0;
    return comp;
}

// Call this function after downloading the audio
var audioFilePath = "/path/to/your/downloaded/audio.mp3"; // Update with actual path
var audioFile = importAudio(audioFilePath);
addAudioToTimeline(audioFile);
```

### Step 4: Combine Everything

You will need to ensure that the JavaScript and ExtendScript communicate effectively. You can use the `evalScript` method in After Effects to run ExtendScript from your HTML panel.

